arguments.report:
## Root cause
`DefaultBinder.bindData` (bind.go:127-133) had a single, unconditional map branch:
```go
if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map {
    for k, v := range data {
        val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))   // always a string
    }
    return nil
}
```
For a `map[string][]string` destination it always stored the *first* value as a plain `string`, so `SetMapIndex` panicked: `reflect.Value.SetMapIndex: value of type string is not assignable to type []string` (confirmed at bind.go:130 by the temp test). Repeated keys could never be preserved.

## Fix (only /echo/bind.go, +10 lines, no other file touched)
```diff
diff --git a/bind.go b/bind.go
index 16c3b7a..eea6379 100644
--- a/bind.go
+++ b/bind.go
@@ -127,6 +127,16 @@ func (b *DefaultBinder) bindData(destination interface{}, data map[string][]stri
 	// Map
 	if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map {
 		for k, v := range data {
+			// if map value is a slice of strings (i.e. map[string][]string) all values for the key must be bound,
+			// otherwise (i.e. map[string]string, map[string]interface{}) only the first value is bound
+			if typ.Elem().Kind() == reflect.Slice && typ.Elem().Elem().Kind() == reflect.String {
+				slice := reflect.MakeSlice(typ.Elem(), len(v), len(v))
+				for i := range v {
+					slice.Index(i).SetString(v[i])
+				}
+				val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), slice)
+				continue
+			}
 			val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))
 		}
 		return nil
```
Slice-of-string values now get a properly typed slice containing **all** values (single-element slice for a single value, all values for a repeated key). `reflect.MakeSlice(typ.Elem(), ...)` also makes named slice value types (e.g. `map[string]StringArray`) work. Every other path (string / interface / struct binding) is byte-for-byte unchanged, so `map[string]interface{}` still binds the first value as a `string` (verified).

